            {
              "id": "CVE-2023-43665",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "python-django: Denial-of-service possibility in django.utils.text.Truncator"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "In Django 3.2 before 3.2.22, 4.1 before 4.1.12, and 4.2 before 4.2.6, the django.utils.text.Truncator chars() and words() methods (when used with html=True) are subject to a potential DoS (denial of service) attack via certain inputs with very long, potentially malformed HTML text. The chars() and words() methods are used to implement the truncatechars_html and truncatewords_html template filters, which are thus also vulnerable. NOTE: this issue exists because of an incomplete fix for CVE-2019-14232."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2023-43665",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2023-43665\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: django\nFixed Version: 3.2.22, 4.1.12, 4.2.6\nLink: [CVE-2023-43665](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2023-43665)\nIn Django 3.2 before 3.2.22, 4.1 before 4.1.12, and 4.2 before 4.2.6, the django.utils.text.Truncator chars() and words() methods (when used with html=True) are subject to a potential DoS (denial of service) attack via certain inputs with very long, potentially malformed HTML text. The chars() and words() methods are used to implement the truncatechars_html and truncatewords_html template filters, which are thus also vulnerable. NOTE: this issue exists because of an incomplete fix for CVE-2019-14232.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2023-43665**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|django|3.2.22, 4.1.12, 4.2.6|[CVE-2023-43665](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2023-43665)|\n\nIn Django 3.2 before 3.2.22, 4.1 before 4.1.12, and 4.2 before 4.2.6, the django.utils.text.Truncator chars() and words() methods (when used with html=True) are subject to a potential DoS (denial of service) attack via certain inputs with very long, potentially malformed HTML text. The chars() and words() methods are used to implement the truncatechars_html and truncatewords_html template filters, which are thus also vulnerable. NOTE: this issue exists because of an incomplete fix for CVE-2019-14232."
